from course_writer import write_course

chapters = {
"README.md": ("GlobeHire tenant-boundary lab", """
This course uses one small GlobeHire workflow to teach a large engineering idea: a route identifier is not just text, but part of the authority for every read and mutation. The worked path begins at the hire-case HTTP route, passes the client string into ChangeHireCaseHandler, parses it as ClientId, and reaches InMemoryHireCaseRepository. That repository keys records by the pair `(ClientId, Guid)`. The aggregate then checks its optimistic Version and records a UTC completion time. Read those layers in that order before changing anything.

The improvement is deliberately a regression suite rather than a production rewrite. Current source already scopes hire-case lookup by client, uses explicit versions, and records completion history. What was missing was an application-level proof that the composition remains safe. The new tests create a case owned by `client-acme`, attempt completion and submission as `client-other`, and verify the owner object is unchanged. A valid owner call must complete exactly one task, retain the fixed UTC clock value, and increment Version exactly once. A stale owner call must throw the stable `concurrency.version_mismatch` business error without altering the second task.

Work through the chapters in order. Keep two mental ledgers: identity scope and state transition. For every call, predict the repository key, returned value, task flags, timestamps, and version before reading the assertion. The project is a teaching system with singleton in-memory repositories and no production authentication, durable database, or distributed concurrency guarantee. The tests establish the behavior of this boundary in one process. They do not turn a route client ID into verified identity. That limitation is part of the exercise: good tests state exactly what they prove.
""", [("src/GlobeHire.Application/HireCases/HireCaseUseCases.cs", "public sealed class ChangeHireCaseHandler", 28)]),
"01-CODEBASE-MAP.md": ("Codebase map: route to aggregate", """
Start in `HireCaseEndpoints.cs`. The route contains `clients/{clientId}/hire-cases/{id}` and passes both values to ChangeHireCaseHandler. The endpoint also parses an If-Match header into an expected integer version. This is the transport boundary: HTTP shapes and status codes belong here, while tenant lookup and lifecycle rules remain below it. Next open `HireCaseUseCases.cs`. CompleteTaskAsync parses the client string, asks IHireCaseRepository for the exact client/id pair, and invokes HireCase.CompleteTask only when a record is found. SubmitAsync follows the same pattern. A null result becomes not-found at the endpoint.

The infrastructure layer is intentionally small. `InMemoryHireCaseRepository` owns a dictionary keyed by `(ClientId ClientId, Guid Id)` and protects it with a lock. The pair matters: a globally valid Guid is insufficient. A request with the right case ID and wrong client produces no aggregate reference, so application code has nothing to mutate. Finally, `HireCase.cs` owns the checklist, Version, Status, and CompletedAt timestamps. CompleteTask checks the expected version before checking the task and uses the injected application clock value supplied by the handler.

The new test file sits in GlobeHire.Application.Tests because the claim spans application, infrastructure, and domain code. Domain-only tests already establish transition rules, while an API happy-path test checks cross-client GET. The new suite fills the middle: it composes the real handler and repository with a fixed clock. Map each assertion to an owner. Repository isolation explains null for the wrong client. Aggregate rules explain version rejection. The clock port explains the exact UTC timestamp. This ownership map prevents tests from accidentally asserting a feature in the wrong layer.
""", [("src/GlobeHire.Infrastructure/HireCases/InMemoryHireCaseRepository.cs", "public sealed class", 35)]),
"02-CONCEPTS.md": ("Concepts: tenant keys, versions, and audit time", """
Tenant isolation is strongest when it is structural. The repository does not retrieve a case by Guid and then ask a caller to remember an authorization check. It uses ClientId and Guid together as the lookup key. That makes the common unsafe path—load by resource ID alone—unavailable through IHireCaseRepository. The handler still does not authenticate the caller; it trusts the client ID passed from the transport. In a production system, authenticated claims or server-side membership would determine that client. Here we test the scope that the project actually implements.

Optimistic versioning protects a different boundary. A correct tenant can still send stale state. HireCase starts at Version 1. Completing a previously incomplete task changes its record and advances the version. A second mutation claiming version 1 must fail before touching another task. Tenant scope answers “which aggregate may this request see?” Version checks answer “which observed state may this request change?” Treating these as separate questions makes failure diagnosis clearer.

Audit time is an input, not a reason to consult the machine clock throughout domain code. ChangeHireCaseHandler receives IClock and passes UtcNow into CompleteTask. The regression uses a FixedClock so the expected CompletedAt is exact and its offset is zero. A wrong-client attempt never reaches that clocked mutation. A stale attempt reaches the aggregate but fails before assigning a timestamp. Those negative assertions matter as much as the success assertion.

The in-memory lock prevents simultaneous dictionary operations from corrupting the collection, but it is not a substitute for database transactions across processes. Likewise, returning null for another tenant avoids revealing whether the Guid exists, but no authentication is proven. Keep these limits beside the concepts so future changes do not overstate a teaching repository’s guarantees.
""", [("src/GlobeHire.Domain/HireCases/HireCase.cs", "public void CompleteTask", 52)]),
"03-WORKED-CHANGE.md": ("Worked change: prove isolation without rewriting it", """
The worked change begins with a fact from current source: IHireCaseRepository.FindAsync requires ClientId and Guid, and the in-memory implementation stores the same pair. Because the production path is already correct, changing it would create needless risk. The useful improvement is to make that contract executable at the application seam.

Create an owned aggregate with deterministic IDs and add it to the real InMemoryHireCaseRepository. Construct ChangeHireCaseHandler with that repository and a fixed clock. First call CompleteTaskAsync with `client-other`, the owned Guid, task `identity`, and version 1. The expected return is null. Then inspect the original owner aggregate: Version remains 1, every IsComplete flag remains false, and every CompletedAt remains null. This checks absence of mutation rather than merely checking the outward response.

For submission, prepare an owner case whose three tasks are complete but whose Status is still Draft. Capture its current version, call SubmitAsync as the other client, and verify null, Draft, and the unchanged version. This fixture is valuable because submission would otherwise be a legal transition; failure therefore comes from tenant lookup rather than an incomplete checklist.

The success case calls completion as `client-acme`. Assert that the returned object is the stored aggregate, only `identity` is complete, its CompletedAt equals the fixed UTC instant, and Version is 2. Finally, complete identity and attempt work-eligibility with stale version 1. Assert the named business error and unchanged second task. Together the four cases form a small matrix: wrong identity with valid version, wrong identity with otherwise valid submit, correct identity and version, and correct identity with stale version. That matrix is the worked artifact.
""", [("tests/GlobeHire.Application.Tests/HireCases/HireCaseTenantIsolationTests.cs", "public sealed class", 70)]),
"04-TESTING-AND-DEBUGGING.md": ("Testing and debugging the boundary", """
Run the smallest test project that owns the composition: `dotnet test tests/GlobeHire.Application.Tests/GlobeHire.Application.Tests.csproj --disable-build-servers -m:1`. The repository targets .NET 10 through global.json. The recorded run used SDK 10.0.302, a single MSBuild worker, and disabled node reuse to fit the constrained machine. Eight application tests passed in the recorded scope.

When a tenant-isolation test fails, classify the symptom before editing. If the wrong-client result is non-null, inspect the arguments passed by ChangeHireCaseHandler and the key used by InMemoryHireCaseRepository. If the result is null but state changed, a lookup or shared fixture may have exposed the same aggregate through another reference. If the valid completion returns but the timestamp differs, check that the handler passes IClock.UtcNow and that the test injected FixedClock. If stale mutation changes state, inspect the ordering inside CompleteTask: EnsureVersion must run before task replacement.

Avoid testing only HTTP status. A 404 can coexist with an earlier mutation if code is ordered badly. These regressions assert both outward outcome and owner state. Also avoid using DateTimeOffset.UtcNow in the fixture, because a broad time assertion can hide local-offset or delayed-write bugs. Exact deterministic time makes the audit claim reviewable.

The test uses the in-memory repository on purpose. It verifies application composition without a database or external service. It does not exercise persistence isolation levels or multiple processes. If a durable repository is added later, reuse this behavior matrix against that implementation and add transaction/concurrency tests appropriate to its storage engine. Do not erase this suite; it remains the fast contract for every implementation.
""", [("tests/GlobeHire.Application.Tests/GlobeHire.Application.Tests.csproj", "<Project", 24)]),
"05-PRACTICE.md": ("Practice: extend the matrix", """
Before coding, write a prediction table with columns for requested client, expected version, requested action, returned value, final status, final version, and changed task. Populate four rows from the worked tests. Then add two proposed rows without implementation: an unknown Guid under the owner client, and a repeated completion of the already-complete identity task using the current version. Explain which layer decides each outcome.

Exercise one is a reading task. Trace the client value from the endpoint route to ClientId.Parse and into the dictionary key. Record every location where the value could be lost or replaced. Decide whether a globally unique case ID would make tenant scope unnecessary. Your answer should explain why defense at the repository contract still reduces accidental cross-client reads even when IDs are hard to guess.

Exercise two is a state task. Starting at Version 1, list the expected version after each successful checklist completion and after submission. Insert a stale work-eligibility attempt after identity. Predict all task timestamps after the failure. Confirm your prediction against the domain code before running tests.

Exercise three is a design task. Sketch a durable repository method with the same `FindAsync(ClientId, Guid)` signature. State where an authenticated principal would become a ClientId, how a database query would include both columns, and how an update would compare the expected version. Do not implement a database or authentication provider for this exercise.

Exercise four is a review task. Imagine a refactor that adds `FindAsync(Guid id)` for an internal batch job. Write a review comment describing the risk, the safer alternative, and a regression that would expose misuse. Your rubric is specific: reference the handler, repository key, aggregate Version, and the outward not-found behavior. General statements about “security” are insufficient.
""", [("src/GlobeHire.Api/HireCases/HireCaseEndpoints.cs", "internal static class", 55)]),
"06-SOLUTIONS-AND-REVIEW.md": ("Solutions and review rubric", """
For the prediction matrix, an unknown Guid and the wrong tenant both return null from the repository and therefore make no state change. Repeating an already-complete task is subtler. CompleteTask checks the expected version first. With the current version it finds the completed task and returns without another increment or timestamp replacement. With an old version it throws before reaching the idempotent branch. This ordering deliberately distinguishes a valid replay based on current state from a stale command.

The route-to-key trace should name HireCaseEndpoints, ChangeHireCaseHandler, ClientId.Parse, IHireCaseRepository.FindAsync, and the dictionary pair in InMemoryHireCaseRepository. A Guid’s uniqueness does not establish authority. Even unguessable identifiers can leak through logs, links, or other responses, and future storage may use predictable keys. Keeping client scope in the repository contract makes an unsafe lookup harder to express.

A durable design should derive tenant identity from trusted server context, then query with both client and case ID. The update should include the observed version or use a concurrency token so a competing change cannot be silently overwritten. Authentication, membership, query scoping, and optimistic concurrency are separate controls; the current teaching app fully demonstrates only repository scoping and in-process aggregate version checks.

Review submissions with four criteria. First, negative tests must inspect unchanged state, not only null or HTTP 404. Second, deterministic clocks must produce an exact UTC timestamp. Third, stale-version coverage must prove the untouched task and unchanged version. Fourth, explanations must state limits: singleton memory storage, no verified principal, and no distributed transaction claim. A strong solution preserves existing production code when evidence shows it is correct. Adding a second unscoped repository method, relying on random IDs, or using broad timing windows should fail review.
""", [("tests/GlobeHire.Domain.Tests/HireCases/HireCaseTests.cs", "public sealed class", 55)]),
"07-TRACE-LAB.md": ("Trace lab: predict every state", """
Use one case with ID `d9d59414-c677-49fb-a9b5-31243fa3ec11`, owner `client-acme`, and three incomplete tasks. At checkpoint A the repository contains one key pair, Status is Draft, Version is 1, and no CompletedAt exists. Do not run code yet.

Trace 1 sends client-other, the same ID, identity, and expected version 1. ClientId.Parse succeeds. FindAsync constructs `(client-other, id)`, which does not match `(client-acme, id)`. The handler’s null-conditional call skips CompleteTask. At checkpoint B, predict the exact same owner state as A. The response cannot distinguish an existing foreign case from a nonexistent case.

Trace 2 sends client-acme with version 1. Lookup returns the owner reference. EnsureVersion succeeds; status is editable; identity is found and incomplete. The record is replaced with IsComplete true and CompletedAt from FixedClock, then Version becomes 2. At checkpoint C only identity changed.

Trace 3 sends client-acme, work-eligibility, and stale version 1. Lookup succeeds, but EnsureVersion observes 2 and throws `concurrency.version_mismatch`. At checkpoint D work-eligibility remains incomplete, its timestamp is null, identity retains its earlier audit, and Version remains 2.

Now create a second fixture with all tasks complete and Version 4 but Status Draft. Trace 4 attempts submit as client-other using version 4. Repository lookup returns null before aggregate validation. Status stays Draft and Version stays 4. Finally submit as the owner: the aggregate finds no incomplete tasks, changes Status to ReadyForReview, and increments Version to 5. Compare your trace against the tests. If any prediction differs, identify whether the misunderstanding concerns lookup scope, null-conditional control flow, validation order, or mutation ownership.
""", [("src/GlobeHire.Domain/HireCases/HireCase.cs", "public sealed class HireCase", 110)]),
"VERIFICATION.md": ("Verification record and limits", """
Verification is intentionally narrow and reproducible. The changed behavior is a new application regression file; production GlobeHire source was not modified because the audited tenant-aware repository and handler were already correct. The test project composes the actual InMemoryHireCaseRepository, ChangeHireCaseHandler, HireCase aggregate, ClientId and WorkforcePlanId value objects, plus a deterministic IClock.

The acceptance cases are: cross-client task completion returns null and preserves all checklist flags, timestamps, and Version; cross-client submission returns null and preserves Draft plus Version even when the checklist would permit submission; an owner completion records the exact UTC clock and increments once; and a stale owner mutation reports `concurrency.version_mismatch` while preserving the target task and current Version. These assertions cover result and state together.

The recorded command uses the repository’s .NET 10 target and the installed 10.0.302 SDK. Single-worker MSBuild and disabled build-server reuse reduce memory pressure. The command restores and builds the targeted Application.Tests dependency graph, then runs eight tests. Evidence files copied beside this chapter contain the exact argv, timestamps, exit code, and full log.

Limits are explicit. This proves in-process behavior of the singleton in-memory repository. It does not prove authentication, authorization membership, a durable transaction, row-level database isolation, multiple-node concurrency, or production policy correctness. ClientId still originates from request data in this teaching API. The fixed clock verifies propagation, not a real time provider. The broader API suite was not rerun because the accepted change only adds application tests and the targeted project already builds the involved source projects. Future durable implementations should reuse the same behavior cases and add storage-specific concurrency tests.
""", [("global.json", "{", 8)]),
}

